\title{[DP4] Train track (2 pts)}
\date{2026-10-27T21:44:36Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\tag{problem}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{You are building a toy train track
  out of a sequence of straight pieces laid end-to-end.  Some pieces
  are one unit long, and some are three units long.  How many
  different ways are there to build a track that is five hundred units
  long?}

\p{For example, there are #{9} different ways to build a track that is #{7} units long, as illustrated below.}

\diagram{500}{*}{\verb<<<|
import Data.List.Split (chunksOf)

segGap = 0.2
railOffset = 0.35

trackSeg n = rails <> ties
  where
    rails = mconcat [ rail # translateY railOffset
                    , rail # translateY (-railOffset)]
    rail = hrule (n' - segGap) # alignL # lw veryThick # lc silver

    k    = 2
    ties = hcat' (with & catMethod .~ Distrib
                       & sep .~ (1/fromIntegral k))
             (replicate (k * n) tie)
           # translateX (1/(2*fromIntegral k) - segGap/2)
    tie  = rect 0.1 1 # lw none # fc (trackColor n)

    n'   = fromIntegral n
trackColor 1 = black
trackColor 3 = orange

drawTrack = hsep segGap . map trackSeg

tracks :: Int -> [[Int]]
tracks n | n < 0 = []
tracks 0 = [[]]
tracks 1 = [[1]]
tracks n = map (1:) (tracks (n-1)) ++ map (3:) (tracks (n-3))

dia :: Diagram B
dia = hsep 1 . map (vsep 0.8) . chunksOf 5
    $ map drawTrack (tracks 7) -- $
<<<}

\hint{Let #{T(n)} denote the number of ways to build a track of length #{n}, and come up with a recurrence for #{T}. What are your choices for the final track segment?}

\hint{To answer this you will need to write a small program. If you use Java, be sure to use \code{BigInteger}.}
